\section{Setting up the diagnostic quizzes, literately}
\label{app:diagnostics}

This appendix is a literate program: the prose and the code are one and
the same source, woven into the text you are reading and tangled into
the files the computer runs.
It implements the diagnostic instruments for the learning-study
evaluation motivated in \cref{conclusions}: pre- and post-tests at the
level of the whole course, and pre- and post-tests around each taught
topic.
Eighteen files are tangled from this section: the course-level pair
[[quiz-course-start.json]] and [[quiz-course-end.json]], and a
start--end pair per topic
([[quiz-funcvars-start.json]]/[[quiz-funcvars-end.json]] and
correspondingly for [[conditionals]], [[repetitions]], [[types]],
[[classes]], [[containers]], [[files]] and [[tracing]]).
The learning platform of the course is Canvas, and the [[canvaslms]]
command-line tool creates the quizzes from their JSON descriptions,
for example:
\begin{minted}{bash}
for f in quiz-*.json; do
  canvaslms quizzes create -c prgi26 -f "$f"
done
\end{minted}

The items are where the catalogue pays off.
Each item targets one critical aspect from the analyses in
\cref{misconceptions}, and --- this is the diagnostic value --- each
distractor encodes a \emph{catalogued misconception}: a student who
picks it does not merely answer wrongly, they tell us which
misconception they hold.
This is the construction principle of concept inventories
\autocite{Julie2020ConceptInventory,KaltakciGurel2015Diagnostics},
here with the distractors grounded in the catalogue rather than
collected anew.

\subsection{Parallel forms drawn at random}
\label{app:diagnostics-forms}

Measuring the same student twice with the same question risks
measuring recognition of the question rather than understanding of the
aspect.
Every aspect therefore has \emph{two} parallel items, A and B: the
critical aspect and the encoded misconceptions are invariant, and only
the surface varies --- names, values, and which choice is correct.
Each aspect enters its quizzes as a \emph{question group}: the group's
pool holds both forms, and Canvas draws one of them at random, per
student and per administration.
Both forms thus appear in both administrations, spread over comparable
random halves of the cohort, so a difficulty difference between the
forms cancels in the aspect's aggregate gain; and since the analysis
records which form each student drew
(\cref{app:diagnostics-analysis}), form effects can be checked rather
than assumed away.
The draws being independent, about half the students meet the same
form twice; for them the recognition risk returns, but it can be
bounded by comparing the same-form with the switched-form pairs --- a
comparison that fixed counterbalanced forms cannot make, because they
assign every student the switched sequence.

The random draw dictates the quiz engine.
Canvas's New Quizzes cannot host it, because their item banks cannot
be created through the API at all; Classic Quizzes' question groups
can, so the quizzes below are Classic Quizzes.
% The Classic question-group support in canvaslms came from our
% upstream feature request, dbosk/canvaslms#341.

\subsection{The course-level quizzes}

The course-level pair brackets the whole course: the start quiz runs in
the first week, the end quiz in the last.
The titles name the course level explicitly, since the parallel
debugging study runs same-shaped concept quizzes in the same course
and the two must stay distinguishable for students and analysis alike.
Each quiz of the pair opens with an ungraded question of its own.
The start quiz opens with the consent item
(\cref{app:diagnostics-ethics}): the quizzes collect research data,
consent must precede collection, and the start quiz is the first of
the eighteen a student meets.
The end quiz opens with whether the student prepared for the quiz.
We ask because preparation would confound the gain measure --- a
student who reviews the material the night before measures
preparation, not what the course left behind.
Each group's name numbers its aspect, with the topic in the tens
digit and the aspect within the topic in the ones digit, so aspects
stay identifiable and stable across all eighteen quizzes and in the
analysis; Canvas orders the groups as listed and sums their points
itself.

<<[[quiz-course-start.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "classic",
  "settings": {
