All quizzes are diagnostics: there is no time limit, a single attempt
suffices, and answers are never shuffled, since the order of the
choices is meaningful in several items (the misconception-encoding
distractor should not accidentally move next to the correct answer).
The [[quiz_type]] among the settings is not the outer [[quiz_type]]
that picks the engine: it is Canvas's category for a Classic Quiz,
where [[assignment]] means a graded quiz.

<<scheduling settings>>=
"time_limit": null,
"due_at": null,
"lock_at": null,
@

<<interaction settings>>=
"quiz_type": "assignment",
"allowed_attempts": 1,
"shuffle_answers": false,
"one_question_at_a_time": false,
@

What the students see afterwards differs between the two ends of a
pair.
A start quiz shows the score only --- never the correct answers, nor
which of the student's own answers were correct --- since its items
return in the matching end quiz.
An end quiz is that pool's \emph{last} administration, so it reveals
the correct answers: the students can review the quiz to study, which
also gives them a reason beyond goodwill to take it seriously.
The one exception to \enquote{last} is deliberate: the course-end
quiz re-draws from the pools that the topic-end quizzes have already
revealed.
By then the revealed answers are simply course material --- studying
them is the learning the course asks for --- and the preparation
question below flags the students who reviewed right before the quiz,
so this path is visible in the analysis rather than silently
confounding it.

<<score-only result view>>=
"show_correct_answers": false,
"hide_results": "always"
@

<<revealing result view>>=
"show_correct_answers": true,
"hide_results": null
@

\subsection{The consent and preparation items}
\label{app:diagnostics-ethics}

The quizzes collect research data, so participation in the study is
opt-in: the consent item below opens the course-level start quiz,
which runs in the first week, before any topic quiz, so the one asked
consent covers all eighteen quizzes and none of the others asks again.
The wording is the same as the parallel debugging study's background
quiz, which opens with it too, so a student who has answered one
recognises the next; each study still filters its extraction by the
consent its own instrument collected (\cref{app:diagnostics-analysis}),
so a \enquote{No} here excludes these eighteen quizzes regardless of what
was answered elsewhere.
A student's grade is unaffected either way: the item carries no
points, grading and teaching run identically regardless of the
answer, and the research extraction filters by consent after the
fact.
The collection was assessed against the Swedish Ethics Review Act
\autocite{Etikprovningslagen} with the institution's checklist and found
not to require review---only ordinary personal data is processed, there
is no intervention, and the teaching is not manipulated for
research---and the institution's research-ethics advisor confirmed the
assessment on 2026-09-01; the assessment accompanies this paper's source
as [[ethics-application-prgi26.md]].
The item therefore links to the participant information page in the
course's learning platform, which describes the purpose, the data, its
handling and the students' rights, and its wording names every source
the two studies use---the parallel debugging study's programming logs
and platform activity included---so that the consent is informed in the
sense the GDPR requires.

The item is a multiple-choice question rather than Canvas's
true/false type because a Classic true/false question cannot relabel
its choices, and the student should answer \enquote{Yes} or
\enquote{No}, not \enquote{True} or \enquote{False} --- the same two
choices the sibling studies' New-Quiz consent item offers, so the
item reads identically wherever a student meets it.
Canvas wants some choice marked correct; the weight on \enquote{Yes}
is that requirement and nothing else --- the item carries no points,
so no answer is more correct than the other, exactly as for the
preparation question below.

<<consent question>>=
{
  "question_name": "Consent",
  "question_text": "<p>I consent to my answers in the course's quizzes and questionnaires, my course submissions, the logs of my programming work that the course's tools record, and my activity in the course's learning platform, being used pseudonymised in computing education research. Recorded sessions happen only if I separately agree to them. My participation is voluntary, does not affect my grade, and I can withdraw at any time by contacting the responsible researcher. I have read <a href='https://canvas.kth.se/courses/63874/pages/information-om-forskningsstudien'>the information page about the study</a>.</p>",
  "question_type": "multiple_choice_question",
  "points_possible": 0,
  "answers": [
    {"answer_text": "Yes", "answer_weight": 100},
    {"answer_text": "No", "answer_weight": 0}
  ]
}
@

The preparation question is ungraded and sits first in every end
quiz, outside any group so that every student gets it.
Canvas wants some choice marked correct even here; the mark on
\enquote{No} is arbitrary and never surfaces, since the question
carries no points.

