\paragraph{Aspect 18: an assignment replaces the old value.}
The critical aspect is that assigning to a variable \emph{replaces} what it
held --- a variable does not \enquote{remember} its old values
\autocite[Table~A.1, p.~359]{Sorva2012Dissertation}.
The both-values distractor encodes the multi-value reading; the first-value
distractor encodes the second assignment as inert (the mirror belief); the
error distractor encodes that a variable, once assigned, cannot be assigned
again.
Form B varies the type (a string), the names and the correct position; the
encoded misconceptions are the same.

