\paragraph{Aspect 54: equality is defined, identity is not.}
The critical aspect separates the two comparisons that aspect~52's
objects invite: \mintinline{python}{==} asks whatever the class's
\mintinline{python}{__eq__} says it asks --- and, without one, falls
back on identity --- whereas \mintinline{python}{is} always asks
whether the two names refer to one and the same object.
Each form defines an \mintinline{python}{__eq__} that compares
\emph{one} attribute and makes two objects that agree on that
attribute but differ in another, so the two comparisons must come
apart: \mintinline{python}{True False}.
A student who answers that way has discerned both halves at once.
The three distractors are the three other ways the pair can fall.
\mintinline{python}{False False} encodes ignoring the user-defined
equality: either \mintinline{python}{==} is taken to compare every
attribute, or the \mintinline{python}{__eq__} is taken to have no
effect, so that \mintinline{python}{==} falls back on identity;
both readings give \mintinline{python}{False}.
\mintinline{python}{True True} encodes aspect~52's misconception
from the other side: that objects which are equal are one object,
the attributes being the identity \autocite{Holland1997}.
\mintinline{python}{False True} encodes swapping the two operators,
reading \mintinline{python}{is} as the comparison of contents and
\mintinline{python}{==} as the comparison of identity.
The course teaches exactly this contrast: the lecture compares two
persons with identical attributes, with and without
\mintinline{python}{__eq__}, and the tutorial prints both
\mintinline{python}{==} and \mintinline{python}{is} for two copies of
one book whose bookmarks differ.

