\paragraph{Aspect 82: assigning to a key replaces or adds.}
The critical aspect is that one and the same assignment does two
things depending on the key: to a key that is there it
\emph{replaces} the value, and to a key that is not there it
\emph{adds} an entry --- so a misspelt key raises no error but silently
adds an entry of its own.
The stem prints after each of the two assignments, which crosses the
two halves of the aspect: a student may get either half wrong
independently, and the four choices are the four combinations.
The duplicated entry encodes taking assignment for appending, so that
the old value stays beside the new one; the [[KeyError]] encodes
carrying the list's behaviour over, where an assignment to an index
that does not exist is an error and the list grows only by
[[append]] --- exactly the contrast the course material draws between
the two containers.
The misspelling is a transposition of two letters in both forms, so
that the new key is plainly a mistake and not a second name.

