\paragraph{Aspect 83: a missing key is an error, unless [[get]] is asked.}
The course material teaches three ways to handle a missing key ---
test with [[in]], catch the [[KeyError]], or ask [[get]] for a
fallback --- and the items take the one that most directly contrasts
with the plain lookup: [[get]] with a fallback value, followed by the
plain lookup of the same missing key.
The critical aspect is that the square brackets treat a missing key as
an error while [[get]] returns the fallback instead; the order of the
two lines matters, since the error must come last for both lines to
be seen.
The [[None]]-after-the-fallback distractor encodes the misconception
that a missing key is silently nothing, also for the square brackets;
the error-at-the-first-line distractor encodes not having discerned
what [[get]] is for, reading it as another spelling of the same
lookup; and the [[None]]-before-the-error distractor encodes confusing
[[get]]'s two documented results, [[None]] without a fallback and the
fallback with one.

