\subsection{More containers (aspects 85--88)}
\label{app:diagnostics-containers}

Four aspects from the analyses of tuples, dictionary keys, sets,
stacks and queues (\cref{containers}).
All four share one difficulty: Python writes the containers alike ---
the same brackets for indexing, the same [[append]], the same
[[for]] --- so the surface does not show which container a program
holds, and every item therefore makes the student predict what the
container \emph{does} rather than recognise how it is written.
The catalogue's fifth aspect, choosing a container by the operations
a program needs, has no item: the course's own choice exercise has no
single key --- the same students can be defensibly kept in a list, a
set or as dictionary keys --- so a multiple-choice item with one
keyed answer would mark wrong what the course accepts.

