\paragraph{Aspect 85: a key must be immutable.}
The critical aspect fuses two earlier ones: a dictionary finds an
entry by its key's value, so the key must be a value that cannot
change afterwards --- a tuple serves, a list does not.
Both forms store one entry under a tuple, read it back, and then try
the same with a list; the program prints once and then stops with a
[[TypeError]] at the list key.
The distractor in which both entries work encodes taking a key to be
as unrestricted as a value, which is also what the belief that a tuple
is merely a list with other brackets predicts.
The distractor in which the tuple fails as well encodes the belief
that only plain values such as strings can be keys, as if a
dictionary were a list indexed by strings.
The [[KeyError]] distractor encodes not discerning a value that
\emph{cannot} be a key from a key that is \emph{absent}, the two
failures the course material sets side by side.
None of the three is documented in the literature we searched; they
are observed in our course material's design, which is why
\cref{containers} analyses them without citations of their own.

