\paragraph{Aspect 86: a tuple cannot be changed.}
The critical aspect is that a tuple is immutable: indexing, [[len]]
and [[for]] work on it as on a list, but assigning to an element
raises a [[TypeError]].
Both forms hold the same pair in a list and in a tuple and assign to
the same index of each, so that only the kind of container varies ---
the course material's own contrast.
Placing a [[print]] between the two assignments separates the
outcomes in time: the list's change is printed before the tuple's
assignment fails.
The changed-tuple distractor encodes the tuple as a list with other
brackets; the unchanged-tuple distractor encodes the tuple as a
\enquote{frozen list} whose writes are silently ignored rather than
refused --- both observed in our course material's design, not yet
documented in the literature.
The error-before-printing distractor encodes reading the program as
a static whole that is checked before it runs, the failure to see that
a program has a dynamic existence besides its static text
\autocite[p.~235]{Sorva2012Dissertation}.

