\paragraph{Aspect 87: a set keeps one of each and no order.}
The critical aspect is what a set \emph{is}: it holds each value once,
and it has no order, so two sets are equal when they hold the same
values.
The item crosses the two features: it prints the size of a set
written with a duplicate, and whether it equals a set written with
the same values, the same duplicate, in another order.
The four answers are then the four combinations of discerning or not
discerning each feature, and which one a student picks tells which
feature they miss.
The ordered distractor (right size, unequal) encodes the set as a list
that happens to drop duplicates, the misconception the course
material's contrast is built against; the counted-duplicate
distractor (wrong size, equal) encodes the set as an unordered heap
that keeps every copy; the list distractor (wrong size, unequal)
encodes not telling the set from a list at all.
None of the three is documented in the computing-education literature
we searched; they are observed in our course material's design.
The forms print a size and a truth value rather than the set itself,
since the order in which a set of strings prints can vary from run to
run.

