\paragraph{Aspect 88: a stack and a queue take from different ends.}
The critical aspect is which element is taken next: a stack gives back
the element added last, a queue the element added first.
The course builds the stack from a list with [[append]] and [[pop]],
and the queue from a [[collections.deque]] with [[append]] and
[[popleft]], so both forms do exactly that, with the same elements
added in the same order to both, and take one element from each.
The containers are named neutrally, as in the course material, since
names like [[stack]] and [[queue]] would say which order to expect.
The swapped distractor encodes confusing the two orders; the
same-end distractors encode carrying one order over to both
containers --- the newest first, or the oldest first.
Choosing a structure without the last-in, first-out property for an
undo function is documented \autocite[p.~173]{Zingaro2018DataStructures},
but as a rare error (7\,\% of the students), and the same authors
report an earlier finding that students do best on questions about
stack and queue interfaces
\autocite[p.~170]{Zingaro2018DataStructures}; the item may therefore
be easy, and its value is in the start quiz, before the unit.

