\paragraph{Aspect 91: an exception skips a close written by hand.}
The critical aspect is that the exception leaves the protected block at
the failing line, so a [[close]] written after it never runs and the
file stays open; [[with]] would have closed it.
The item lets the student ask the file itself, through [[f.closed]],
as the course material does.
The closed distractor encodes the belief that the rest of the
[[try]] block still runs --- what \textcite{RashkovitsLavy2012} found
students had \textcquote[343]{RashkovitsLavy2012}{not assimilated};
the nothing-more distractor encodes the other half of the same
sentence, that after the handler the program \enquote{continues from
there}.
The converted-word distractor credits [[int]] with understanding the
word, in the spirit of what \textcite{Pea1986Superbug} calls the
superbug; Pea does not report it for conversions, so this is our
reading.
Form B varies the file, its word and the message.

