\paragraph{Aspect 92: a missing file is an exception from open.}
The critical aspect is that opening a file that does not exist raises
[[FileNotFoundError]] in [[open]], before anything is read, and that
the exception travels out of the reading function to the caller's
handler --- where the course material catches it, since the caller
knows what to do instead.
The stuck-in-the-function distractor encodes the difficulty with
\textcquote[350]{RashkovitsLavy2012}{handling exceptions further up the
calling chain}; the nothing-more distractor encodes, as in aspect~91,
not knowing that the program continues after the handler.
The got-the-text distractor encodes reading a missing file as an empty
one; we found no study of this belief, and it is in the bank because
the course material contrasts the missing file with the existing one.
Form B varies the names and the messages.

