\subsection{Analysing the results}
\label{app:diagnostics-analysis}

Canvas compiles each quiz's responses into a student-analysis report:
a CSV file with one row per student and one column per pooled
question, headed by the question's Canvas id and text.
The analysis program, [[analyze_diagnostics.py]], fetches those
reports itself --- we know the course and the quiz titles, so nothing
is clicked or downloaded by hand --- using the same [[canvaslms]]
machinery as the command-line tool.

The program does not carry its own answer key.
It reads the tangled quiz files: they define every quiz's title, every
aspect's group, and every question's choices and correct answer, and
they are tangled from the same source as this prose, so the key cannot
drift from the instrument.
Answers are paired across the two administrations by the aspect
number in the group's name (11--62), which is what the two forms of an
aspect share; which form Canvas drew for a student is read off the
form suffix of the question they answered.
The program writes one tidy CSV row per student, quiz and aspect ---
quiz, student, aspect number and label, the drawn form, the chosen
answer's position and text, and whether it is correct --- and prints
per-aspect summary tables: how many students answered correctly before
and after, and how the wrong answers distribute over the choices.
The distribution over wrong answers is the diagnostic payoff: each
distractor encodes a catalogued misconception, so the table shows
\emph{which} misconceptions a cohort holds, not merely how many
students err.
Only consenting students' rows enter any of it: every quiz's report
is filtered by the consent collected in the course-level start quiz
(\cref{app:diagnostics-ethics}), and the student column carries the
login ID rather than the name --- both arrangements have their own
subsections below.
% TODO(#29): add learnlog instrumentation (the vt-debug episodes.nw
% pattern).

